# Blog posts, part 3
BLOG.append({
"slug": "grow-your-business-without-choking-cash-flow",
"title": "How to Grow Your Business Without Choking Your Cash Flow | Loan Ready",
"h1": "How to grow your business without choking your cash flow",
"desc": "A practical growth funding guide for Palm Beach County and Treasure Coast owners: match money to its purpose, build real banking relationships, track the right numbers, and plan your next two rounds.",
"lede": "Growth eats cash. Here's how to fund it so you scale up without trading your margins to expensive money.",
"service": ("/grow-your-business/", "Grow Your Business"),
"tag": "Grow Your Business",
"body": """
<p>Growth is the goal, and it's also one of the most common reasons good businesses run out of cash. You land a bigger contract, hire ahead of demand, buy inventory for the busy season or open a second location, and suddenly money is going out months before it comes back in.</p>
<p>The owners who grow well aren't just good at selling. They fund growth deliberately. This guide covers the principles we use with clients across Palm Beach County and the Treasure Coast to plan growth that doesn't choke cash flow.</p>

<h2>Why growth eats cash</h2>
<p>Most businesses pay for growth before they get paid for it. A contractor buys materials and pays crews weeks before the draw arrives. A distributor stocks inventory months before it sells. A medical practice hires staff before the patient base fills their schedules. That gap between cash out and cash in is your working capital need, and it grows as you grow.</p>
<p>When owners don't plan for it, they fill the gap with whatever is fastest, usually credit cards or a merchant cash advance. Fast money is expensive money, and expensive money eats the margin that was supposed to fund the next step.</p>

<h2>Rule 1: Match the money to its purpose</h2>
<p>The most common funding mistake is financing long-term assets with short-term money. A simple guide:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Seasonal swings and working capital:</b> a business line of credit. Draw when you need it, pay it down when receivables come in.</li>
<li><b>Equipment and vehicles:</b> equipment financing with a term that roughly matches the equipment's useful life.</li>
<li><b>Expansion, acquisitions and refinancing expensive debt:</b> a term loan, often SBA 7(a) for longer terms.</li>
<li><b>Real estate:</b> long-term commercial mortgages or SBA 504.</li>
<li><b>A true emergency or a fast opportunity:</b> short-term funding as a bridge, with a planned exit into cheaper money.</li>
</ul>
<p>When the term of the money matches the life of what it pays for, the investment pays for itself instead of draining daily cash. You can compare payments for each type in our <a href="/loan-calculator/">loan calculator</a>.</p>

<h2>Rule 2: Build banking relationships before you need them</h2>
<p>The best time to meet a banker is when you don't need money. Where you bank, and how you bank, quietly shapes what you can borrow later:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Choose a bank that lends to businesses like yours,</b> not just one with a convenient branch. Community banks and credit unions in Palm Beach County often have more flexibility than national banks for local owners.</li>
<li><b>Keep your deposits there.</b> Your deposit history is one of the best underwriting tools a bank has. Splitting revenue across five accounts makes your business harder to read.</li>
<li><b>Get to know a relationship manager</b> and send them your financials once a year, even when you're not asking for anything.</li>
<li><b>Keep the account clean:</b> no NSFs, no negative days, no personal spending.</li>
</ul>
<p>Owners who do this get treated as clients. Owners who show up only when they're desperate get treated as applications.</p>

<h2>Rule 3: Watch the numbers lenders watch</h2>
<p>You don't need a finance degree, but you should know these numbers every month:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Gross margin:</b> what's left from each sale after direct costs. If growth lowers your margin, it may not be worth it.</li>
<li><b>Debt service coverage:</b> cash flow compared to debt payments. Many lenders want 1.25x or better.</li>
<li><b>Days sales outstanding:</b> how long customers take to pay. Faster collections are the cheapest financing there is.</li>
<li><b>Cash runway:</b> how many weeks you could operate on cash on hand.</li>
<li><b>Customer concentration:</b> if one customer is more than a quarter of revenue, lenders will notice, and so should you.</li>
</ul>
<p>Current monthly books make all of this visible. Without them, you're steering by feel. Our <a href="/bookkeeping-tax-planning/">bookkeeping and tax team</a> builds these reports every month.</p>

<h2>Rule 4: Plan your next two rounds, not just this one</h2>
<p>Every financing decision affects the next one. Taking a high-cost advance today may solve this month and block the bank line you'll need next year. Over-writing off expenses saves taxes in April and shrinks the loan you qualify for in June.</p>
<p>We map growth funding 12 to 24 months ahead. What will you need, when, and for what? Which lender fits each step? What does the file need to look like by then? Then every move, from how you file taxes to when you apply, supports the plan.</p>

<h2>Rule 5: Build credit capacity in the business</h2>
<p>A strong business credit profile, separate from your personal credit, gives you more ways to fund growth: vendor terms, business cards and lines that don't depend entirely on your personal score. With the right credit profile and financial footprint, some owners also qualify for 0% introductory-rate business credit options, which can be a powerful, low-cost tool when used with a payoff plan. We cover the foundations in <a href="/blog/how-to-build-business-credit/">how to build business credit</a>.</p>

<h2>Signs you're ready to grow, and signs to wait</h2>
<p><b>You're likely ready if:</b> your gross margin is stable or rising, your books are current, you have at least a few weeks of cash runway, demand is consistent rather than one-time, and your debt payments are comfortably covered by cash flow.</p>
<p><b>Consider waiting, or fixing the foundation first, if:</b> you're covering payroll with advances, your books are months behind, one customer makes up most of your revenue, or your personal credit has slipped in the last year. Growth on a shaky foundation magnifies the cracks. Fixing them first usually takes a few months, and it lets you grow with cheaper money when you do.</p>

<h2>What well-funded growth looks like</h2>
<p>Ragtop is a good example. The business had real demand but was buried in merchant cash advances and high-interest debt. Rather than add another advance, we built a plan: six loans over six months, each one cheaper than the money it replaced. Every MCA was paid off, and with cash flow freed up, the owner opened a new division that now does more than $2 million a year.</p>
<p>That's the difference between being funded and being financed well.</p>

<h2>How Loan Ready helps you grow</h2>
<p>Our <a href="/grow-your-business/">growth and funding strategy</a> work brings together everything a growing business needs: credit repair, bookkeeping, tax planning, banking guidance and funding through iFinancial, which has arranged more than $50 million for over 200 clients. We help you decide where to bank, how to build long-term lender relationships, and which financing fits each stage. Then we handle the heavy lifting.</p>
<p>Ready to plan your next stage? Take the free <a href="/bankable-check/">Bankable Check</a> or visit us at 751 Northlake Blvd, Suite 2D in North Palm Beach.</p>
""",
"faqs": [
 ("What's the best way to fund business growth?", "Match the financing to the purpose: lines of credit for working capital, equipment financing for equipment, term or SBA loans for expansion, and long-term mortgages for real estate. Avoid funding long-term growth with short-term, high-cost money."),
 ("When should I talk to a bank about a loan?", "Before you need one. Build the relationship early, keep your deposits and financials with them, and share your plans. You'll get better terms and faster answers when the time comes."),
 ("Do you work with businesses outside Palm Beach County?", "Yes. Our office is in North Palm Beach, and we work with owners across the Treasure Coast and beyond by phone and video."),
],
})

BLOG.append({
"slug": "how-to-build-business-credit",
"title": "How to Build Business Credit: A Step-by-Step Guide | Loan Ready",
"h1": "How to build business credit (and stop using your personal credit for everything)",
"desc": "Step-by-step guide to building business credit in Florida: business credit scores, setting up your foundation, accounts that report, mistakes to avoid, and how long it takes.",
"lede": "A step-by-step guide to building a business credit profile that lenders and vendors take seriously.",
"service": ("/grow-your-business/", "Grow Your Business"),
"tag": "Business Credit",
"body": """
<p>Most small business owners fund their companies on personal credit: personal cards, personal loans, personal guarantees on everything. It works until it doesn't. One big purchase or one tough quarter can drag your personal score down and block both your business and your family from good financing.</p>
<p>Business credit is a separate profile tied to your company, not your Social Security number. Building it takes time, but it gives you more financing options, better vendor terms and a cleaner line between your business and personal finances. Here's how to do it.</p>

<h2>What business credit is</h2>
<p>Business credit bureaus track how your company pays its bills. The three main ones are Dun &amp; Bradstreet, Experian Business and Equifax Business. Each scores differently:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Dun &amp; Bradstreet PAYDEX:</b> a 1–100 score based on payment timeliness. Around 80 means you pay on time, and paying early can push it higher.</li>
<li><b>Experian Intelliscore Plus:</b> a 1–100 score that blends payment history, credit use, public records and company information.</li>
<li><b>Equifax business scores:</b> include payment and risk scores based on trade and financial data.</li>
</ul>
<p>Unlike personal credit, business credit reports are generally available to anyone who pays for them, including vendors, lenders, landlords and potential partners.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Set up a real, consistent business foundation</h2>
<p>Lenders and bureaus need to see a legitimate, established business. Start here:</p>
<ul class="checks">
<li>Form an LLC or corporation and keep it active with the Florida Division of Corporations (Sunbiz)</li>
<li>Get an EIN from the IRS (it's free)</li>
<li>Open a business bank account and run all business money through it</li>
<li>Use a business address and phone number, and list them the same way everywhere</li>
<li>Get any required local business tax receipts and licenses</li>
<li>Build a simple website and business email on your own domain</li>
</ul>
<p>Consistency matters. If your business name, address and phone are listed differently on Sunbiz, your bank, your website and your Google profile, bureaus may create split or incomplete files.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Get your D-U-N-S number</h2>
<p>Dun &amp; Bradstreet assigns a free D-U-N-S number that anchors your business file with them. Many lenders, vendors and government programs use it. You can request one directly from Dun &amp; Bradstreet at no cost. You don't need to buy their paid packages to get one.</p>

<h2>Step 3: Open accounts that report to business bureaus</h2>
<p>Business credit is built by paying accounts that report your history. Not every vendor reports, so ask before you open an account:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Vendor or trade accounts (net-30):</b> suppliers that let you buy now and pay within 30 days, and that report to one or more business bureaus. Office supply, shipping, fuel and industry suppliers are common starting points.</li>
<li><b>A business credit card</b> that reports to business bureaus. Many still require a personal guarantee, but the account history builds your business profile.</li>
<li><b>Equipment leases and loans</b> often report as well.</li>
</ul>
<p>Start with a few accounts and use them regularly for things you'd buy anyway. You don't need dozens.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Pay early, not just on time</h2>
<p>Payment history is the biggest factor in business credit. With PAYDEX in particular, paying on the due date earns a solid score, while paying early can earn a higher one. Set reminders or autopay, and keep your business card balances reasonable. High utilization can hurt business scores just as it hurts personal ones.</p>

<h2>Step 5: Monitor and correct your business reports</h2>
<p>Errors happen on business reports too: wrong addresses, outdated owners, accounts that belong to a similarly named company. Check your files with each bureau periodically and correct anything inaccurate. Make sure your company information matches what's on Sunbiz and your bank records.</p>

<h2>How long it takes</h2>
<p>With a solid foundation and three to five reporting accounts paid early, many businesses establish a meaningful profile within about six months to a year. A strong profile that unlocks larger unsecured lines usually takes longer, and lenders will still consider time in business and revenue.</p>

<h2>Mistakes to avoid</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Mixing personal and business spending.</b> It weakens your business file and complicates your books and taxes.</li>
<li><b>Paying for "instant business credit" packages.</b> Credit can't be bought. It's earned by paying real accounts over time.</li>
<li><b>Using a CPN or someone else's credit.</b> Never. It can be illegal and can destroy your ability to borrow.</li>
<li><b>Ignoring personal credit.</b> Most small business lenders still review the owner's personal credit, especially for larger loans. Build both.</li>
<li><b>Letting your Sunbiz annual report lapse.</b> An inactive entity is a red flag to lenders and vendors.</li>
</ul>

<h2>A 90-day business credit starter plan</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Days 1–15:</b> confirm your LLC or corporation is active on Sunbiz, get your EIN, open a dedicated business bank account, and make your business name, address and phone identical everywhere.</li>
<li><b>Days 15–30:</b> request your free D-U-N-S number, set up a business phone listing and a simple website, and check for existing files with Experian Business and Equifax Business.</li>
<li><b>Days 30–60:</b> open two or three net-30 vendor accounts that report to business bureaus, and use them for purchases you already make.</li>
<li><b>Days 60–90:</b> pay every invoice early, add a business credit card that reports to business bureaus, keep balances low, and pull your business reports to confirm the accounts are showing.</li>
</ul>
<p>After 90 days, keep paying early, add accounts gradually, and review your reports every quarter. Steady, boring consistency is what builds a strong business credit profile.</p>

<h2>Business credit and bankability</h2>
<p>Business credit is one piece of a bankable footprint. Banks look at your business credit, your personal credit, your financial statements, your tax returns and your bank statements together. When all of them agree, you get more options and better terms, including, for strong profiles, 0% introductory-rate business credit options.</p>
<p>That's what Loan Ready does. We help owners build business credit alongside <a href="/credit-repair/">personal credit repair</a>, <a href="/bookkeeping-tax-planning/">bookkeeping and tax planning</a>, and a funding plan with iFinancial. DMLPA is a good example: after repairing credit and paying off high-interest cards and MCA debt, the business opened three lines of credit and is now working on a 10-year SBA loan.</p>
<p>Want to see where your business stands? Take the free <a href="/bankable-check/">Bankable Check</a> or learn how we help you <a href="/grow-your-business/">grow your business</a>.</p>
""",
"faqs": [
 ("Does business credit affect my personal credit?", "Generally, accounts reported only to business bureaus don't appear on your personal report. But if you personally guarantee a debt and default, it can affect your personal credit. Many business cards also check your personal credit when you apply."),
 ("How do I check my business credit score?", "You can request your business reports and scores from Dun & Bradstreet, Experian Business and Equifax Business. Some offer limited free access, and full reports are usually paid."),
 ("Do I need an LLC to build business credit?", "A registered LLC or corporation with its own EIN, bank account and consistent contact information is the standard foundation. Sole proprietors have a much harder time separating business credit from personal credit."),
],
})


def guide_cards(posts=None, n=None):
    ps = (posts or BLOG)[: n or len(posts or BLOG)]
    return '<div class="grid-3 guides">' + "".join(
        f'<a class="guide-card" href="/blog/{p["slug"]}/"><span class="g-tag">{p["tag"]}</span><h3>{p["h1"][0].upper() + p["h1"][1:]}</h3><p>{p["lede"]}</p><span class="go">Read the guide →</span></a>'
        for p in ps) + "</div>"

